Operations on virtual hard disks can't be performed with the VM running. Deallocate your VM with az vm deallocate. 
The following example deallocates the VM named myVM in the resource group named myResourceGroup:

az vm deallocate --resource-group Nexora --name getnexora

The VM must be deallocated to expand the virtual hard disk. Stopping the VM with az vm stop doesn't release the compute resources. 
To release compute resources, use az vm deallocate.
View a list of managed disks in a resource group with az disk list. 
The following example shows a list of managed disks in the resource group named myResourceGroup:

az disk list --resource-group Nexora --query '[*].{Name:name,size:diskSizeGB,Tier:sku.tier}' --output table 

Name                                                 Size    Tier
---------------------------------------------------  ------  -------
getnexora_OsDisk_1_f47723989de447d6a5d6100caffb42e4  30      Premium

Expand the required disk with az disk update. The following example expands the managed disk named myDataDisk to 50 GB:

az disk update --resource-group Nexora --name getnexora_OsDisk_1_f47723989de447d6a5d6100caffb42e4 --size-gb 50 

When you expand a managed disk, the updated size is rounded up to the nearest managed disk size.
Start your VM with az vm start. The following example starts the VM named myVM in the resource group named myResourceGroup:

az vm start --resource-group Nexora --name getnexora

